1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Selection asks for one rank, not the whole order: given n keys, return the k-th smallest.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Sorting first answers every rank at once, and the bill is Theta of n log n comparisons.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Quickselect partitions around a pivot and recurses into one side only: about three n comparisons on average.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Its worst case is quadratic. This lesson builds the pivot that removes that worst case.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
For the minimum, n minus one comparisons are necessary: every key except one must lose a comparison.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
n minus one comparisons also suffice, so linear time is optimal for selection.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The adversary answers each comparison to keep two candidates alive, so n minus two comparisons cannot decide.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The sorting lower bound of n log n is a bound on sorting, not on selection.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Quickselect: partition around a pivot, then recurse into the side that holds rank k.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
One level costs n minus one comparisons and throws the other side away completely.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A pivot near the middle halves the problem: n plus n over 2 plus n over 4 is at most 2n.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Randomised over 20 seeded runs at n = 1024: 3.25n comparisons on average, 4.61n at worst.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Sorted input, pivot equal to the first element, and k equal to n: each partition peels off one key.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The costs are n minus 1, then n minus 2, and so on: n(n-1)/2 comparisons. At n = 64 that is 2,016.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Asking for the middle is no better on this input: 1,520 comparisons at n = 64 against the 2,016 bound.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Nothing in this input is random, so no average-case promise applies. A deterministic pivot is needed.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Median of medians, step one: cut the 25 keys into five groups of five, in input order.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Sorting each group of five costs at most 7 comparisons, a constant, so all the groups cost O(n).

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The group medians of this array are 17, 29, 19, 21 and 27.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Step two: select the median of those five medians, using the same algorithm recursively.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
The recursive call is on 5 keys, so the pivot costs a fifth of the input, not the whole of it.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
21 is the median of the medians. Here it is also the median of all 25, which is luck, not a promise.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Step three: partition the whole array around 21, exactly as quicksort would.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Twelve keys are smaller and twelve larger, so rank 13 is the pivot itself and the search ends.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Why 21 must sit near the middle: at least half of the group medians are greater than or equal to it.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Each of those groups then contributes at least three keys greater than or equal to its median.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
So at least 3n/10 minus 6 keys lie on each side. Here the true counts are 12 and 12.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The larger side holds at most 7n/10 plus 6 keys, so the recursion is at most seven tenths of the input.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
T(n) is at most T(n/5) plus T(7n/10) plus O(n): a fifth for the pivot, seven tenths to recurse.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
A fifth plus seven tenths is nine tenths, so the level sums shrink geometrically: n, 0.9n, 0.81n.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
The geometric sum is at most ten n, so T(n) is O(n): the first worst-case linear selection.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Measured comparisons: 103 at n = 25, 7,107 at n = 1,000 and 29,505 at n = 4,096.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
With groups of size g, each side is guaranteed at least (g+1)/(4g) of the input.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
g = 5 gives one fifth plus seven tenths, which is 0.9. g = 7 gives 6/7. Both shrink.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
g = 3 gives one third plus two thirds, exactly 1, so the levels do not shrink: Theta of n log n.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Solving both recurrences: T(n)/n settles near 8.5 for groups of 5 and keeps climbing for groups of 3.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
GATE 2019: the median of medians of n arrays of n keys costs Theta of n squared.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
GATE 2008: a pivot that leaves at least n/5 on each side gives T(n) at most T(n/5) + T(4n/5) + n.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
GATE 2006: if the median can be found in O(n), quicksort with the median as pivot is Theta of n log n.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Three questions, one idea: a guaranteed good pivot is what removes the quadratic case.

